  "title": "The long-ant crowd, late in a long session -- and is the stuck thing the little ones?",
  "question": "Watch both, then tell me two things: which one looks less stuck, and -- in whichever you are looking at -- is the thing that reads as stuck the SHORT single-blob ants crowded together, or the LONG seven-segment ones standing still?",
  "context_md": "Your report, 2026-09-11: *\"long ants getting stuck. Not all of them but it happens regularly. It seems like they get stuck in a big group/pile of long ants.\"*\n\n**Three things changed since the pair you marked up.**\n\n**1. The format.** This is a scrubbable frame sequence, not a GIF -- the page plays it on its own timer, so it runs about **4.4x real speed** in a 9-second loop instead of the 51-second crawl you got last time. Arrow keys step it; space plays and pauses.\n\n**2. I answered your three markers, and they were not what either of us thought.** All three were **full-length long ants that are not stuck at all**. Seven, seven and six segments; three, one and one directions they could legally walk; and across the whole 3,000-frame loop not one of them tried a step and failed -- they simply never asked to move. One of them was carrying food and *gaining* energy while it stood there. So they were never in any of the pile numbers I put under that card, in either arm. That is a third problem, it is filed, and nothing in this change touches it.\n\n**3. The window moved, and so did the world.** Your pair was frames 28,000-31,000, where the biggest clump on the bed is four animals -- so the big numbers I printed under it came from elsewhere in the run and did not describe the picture you were looking at. That was my mistake. This pair is frames 117,000-119,400, late in a long session, where the crowding actually happens, and every number below is measured **in this window**. A separate change (nest smell) also landed on the trunk in between, so these are new worlds, not re-renders.\n\n**What the change does.** A carrying long ant with nowhere to go waits for the nestmate in its way rather than turning round, and it used to wait for ever because that nestmate was usually stuck for the same reason. One arm still waits for ever; the other gives it four beats and turns round.\n\n**Why I am asking about the little ones.** Of the animals wedged in a pile here, most have bodies of a single cell -- and their own inherited body plan says one cell, so they were born that way rather than cut down. For a one-cell body, turning round is not a move; there is nothing to turn. Clumps counting only long bodies never exceed three animals in either arm. If what you are pointing at is the small ones, the next round is about why the colony is breeding them; if it is the long ones standing still, it is about why they stop asking to walk. I cannot tell those apart from here and you can.\n\nThe frame is the right-hand third of the bench, which is where most of the colony is in both arms (97% of one, 62% of the other -- the rest of that one is a second group off the left edge).",
